ting  factories that could mean higher meat  prices for Canadians in the short term  but in the long term the Canadian meat  Council says there may be bigger changes  coming when I said to a few few people  in government is what the plants look  like precoded are not what they look  like currently and I don't think that's  what the plants will look like post  Kovac what I do think it has done is  advanced the discussion of whether or  not you would want to look at having  more automation in your plants and I  think on balance most of the plants are  looking at that at some level this might  be a sneak peek at the future at this  pork plant in Denmark machines do much  of the cutting that humans do in North  America Tyson's always been a company  that's been out on the forefront of  innovation just last year u.s. meat  processing giant Tyson Foods opened this  sparkling new facility in Arkansas to  develop new technology automating many  of its production processes  and in 2015 jbs bought scott  technologies a new zealand company that  specializes in robot butchers we  developed the world's first lamb x-ray  primal and this promotional video from  here at the jbs lamb processing plant in  australia shows how the company uses  sophisticated technology like artificial  intelligence to automate some meat  cutting jobs that humans used to do does  that by x-raying  every animal as an individual animal and  identifying where the fourth and fifth  rib are and where that hindquarter cut  is and it's the whole purpose of that  x-ray primal machine x-rays and CT  scanners are used to create a 3d model  of the shape and size of the carcass  before the cutting starts Scott  technologies claims this results in 90%  accuracy the machines are also faster  and over time these robot butchers keep  getting better at their job and unlike  humans they don't get sick or injured  and they don't need breaks or benefits  the technology has already significantly  reduced the number of workers on the  cutting lines Automation could make meat  plants much safer when the next virus  hits it could also mean fewer jobs but  as the Quezada family tries to find  small moments of joy without their  husband and father they're not thinking  about that now my family is devastated  and this time is very hard every day  every day it's very hard they're just  trying to come to terms with the loss  with me from Fort Collins Colorado is  Temple Grandin she's a professor of  animal science at Colorado State  University she's an author inventor  lecturer and designer of livestock  handling facilities all over North  America her thinking process is based  more on visual images than words and her  autism gives her a unique ability to  understand how animals see the world and  her skills have helped meat processors  and slaughterhouses handle animals in a  more humane way and she's a female  trailblazer in a mostly male industry  Temple Grandin thanks for joining us  given what you know about meat  processing plants how many people work  there  how closely they work hundreds of  workers who have become sick with Co bid  19 what grade do you give the industry  for how its responded to the pandemic  well I've actually worked hard to  respond to it  Kovac attacks where people are crammed  together whether it's cruise ships  prisons aircraft carriers any place  where they're together and actually the  the plants seem to be recovering now I  just talked to on two-port plants two  days ago and they tested everybody in  the plant and there were many positives  but the interesting thing is is that  half of those positives had no symptoms  so something's going on here with the  virus but the virus attacks people that  are indoors close together no matter  where it is yeah there's so many  unanswered questions still about how  this virus spreads and how its  transmitted meat-processing I know you  know has changed so much in the last few  decades you've been a part of that it's  al