ight here in Fort  Collins where I live we've got the great  big Coors plant but then you have all  these little tiny craft breweries and I  think it's gonna be more local things  like this you will when I first went in  the industry in Arizona we had our two  big ones they would cut a hay in swift's  and then we had a bunch of what I call  medium-sized plants so we had Arizona  beef we had her says Arizona beef might  do 400 Kathlyn a it was not a locker  plant but the to all the other smaller  plants are maybe one-third of the beef  supply now those little plants the  smaller plants have have on they  couldn't compete with the big guys  so what's it happen in other industries  like beer for example is you don't try  to compete with the big guys you do  something that's special so if there are  there are micro breweries now that are  doing very well you're suggesting micro  meat processing well that's actually  some of this is starting to happen in  fact I went to New Zealand two years ago  and they have rather small plants to  call boutique plants and they'll put out  very specific orders a hundred boxes of  some special cut to send to Japan for  example so if we want meat processing  that is both humane as possible for the  animals and safe as possible for the  human workers do you think we're going  to have to prepare to pay more for me  big concentrated supply chains are  fragile the paradox is big is very  efficient big can do a really good job  food safety animal welfare whatever but  it's fragile and if you go to a more  distributed supply chain it's gonna be  less prone to disruption I don't care  what product it is but it's gonna cost  more that's the paradox I don't care  what product it is meat semiconductors  pharmaceuticals pharmaceutical supply  chain the big really big example of big  is fragile just one more question about  the industry I know you're closely  connected to the the meat industry do  you think they have a handle now on the  spread of Co vid 19 and or do you think  we might continue to see outbreaks in  these processing plants I think the  plants have got a handle on it now we're  going to get outbreaks son what's gonna  happen in the restaurants who knows  that's a bigger worry right now then the  plants I think that's gotten under  control there the weekly slaughter rates  are going back up that's a very good  sign that they have a handle on it I'm  looking at my cattle buyers weekly and  they said that the capacity of the  plants they're ramping it up that means  they're getting it under control  they would some of these plants got down  to half capacity you know now the  industry is running about eighty ninety  percent now that's actually good  [Music]

